1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. The desert people saw — a
calendar.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:09,000
Desert seasons are hard to tell from the
land — so people read the stars.

3
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:28,000
Around June 7, al-Thurayyā (the
Pleiades) first appears before dawn, and
summer begins.

4
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:36,000
The rainy season starts — al-Wasm, “the
brand.” The first rain brands dry land
green.

5
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:41,000
Legend says this rain becomes pearls at
sea — truffles on sand.

6
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:49,000
Before dawn, the Scorpion’s head rises
in the east as the Pleiades set in the
west.

7
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:09,000
Before Islam, people believed stars
setting at dawn brought rain — the
Pleiades most of all.

8
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:14,000
December 7 starts the desert’s forty
coldest days: al-Murabbaʿāniyya.

9
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:20,000
“When the Pleiades rise at nightfall,
the shepherd looks for a cloak.”

10
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:26,000
This month, the Pleiades are already up,
with red Aldebaran below.

11
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Winter solstice: a year measured by a
stick.

12
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

